WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.839
Tucked away in a valley in the Allegheny Mountains
in West Virginia, is this:

00:00:04.839 --> 00:00:07.029
the Green Bank Radio Telescope,

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:10.030
the largest steerable radio telescope
in the world.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:12.290
Now if you're building an optical telescope,

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:14.059
something that looks at the stars,

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:15.059
then you want to be on top of a mountain,

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:18.610
you want to get through as much of Earth's
atmosphere as you can.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.900
But a radio telescope needs to be isolated

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.300
from all the radio noise that humanity produces.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.720
Now, one way is to build it in the middle
of a load of mountains.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.140
Solid rock does a pretty good job of shielding.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.010
But another way is to get everyone around
you to just shut up.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:37.199
Welcome to the National Radio Quiet Zone.

00:00:37.199 --> 00:00:40.489
...er, this is where I would have used a big,
sweeping drone shot

00:00:40.489 --> 00:00:42.020
to get a dramatic picture.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:45.100
But remote control isn't allowed here.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:48.739
The job of the Green Bank telescope is actually
really, really flexible,

00:00:48.739 --> 00:00:50.690
from looking at objects in the solar system

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:54.699
to looking at objects halfway across the universe
and further.

00:00:54.699 --> 00:00:59.019
It was instrumental in uncovering the molten
core of Mercury.

00:00:59.019 --> 00:01:01.979
It helped resolve a sort of controversy in
astronomy

00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:04.040
about the distance to a nearby star cluster.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.250
And that might sound a little bit mundane,

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.220
but measuring distances in astronomy is very
hard.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:11.440
And one of the biggest projects the telescope
does right now

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.260
is also looking for living things out there
in space.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:15.860
It’s called the Breakthrough Listen project,

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:19.480
and it’s a search for extraterrestrial intelligence
using the Green Bank telescope

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.900
and other facilities like it throughout the
world.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.170
The Quiet Zone is roughly a rectangle,

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:25.970
about 100 miles on each side.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:27.290
And since 1958,

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:31.590
the US Government has put strict limits on
transmissions within the Zone.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:35.600
Partly for this telescope, and partly because
of a much more secure military facility

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.040
thirty miles away that listened to
more earthly communications.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:41.380
Some folks writing about the Quiet Zone,

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.840
they say that all transmissions are banned
for the whole hundred miles,

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.300
but that's not true: there are cities within
the wider parts of the Zone,

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.040
and the folks there happily use
wifi and cell phones.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.300
It's the Quiet Zone, not the Silent Zone.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.170
Out there, the rules are about big transmitters,

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.150
the kind that put out TV and radio signals:

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:00.650
they're required to use low-powered,

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:03.900
directional antennas rather than just spraying
signal everywhere.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.710
Within ten miles of here, though,

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:08.289
the rules start becoming a bit more strict.

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:09.670
This telescope is sensitive enough that,

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:11.039
if it was pointed the right way,

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:15.610
it could pick up a signal with the equivalent
energy of one snowflake hitting the ground.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.469
And even if it's not pointed right at you,

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:20.140
it's got two acres of surface area

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.310
to hear your phone screaming for cell towers
that just aren't there.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:28.650
Living in a place without a cell phone is
definitely an interesting change.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.680
Before two years ago, I did have a cell phone,

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.400
could look up anything I wanted on Google.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.569
You know, after a week of living here?

00:02:35.569 --> 00:02:37.470
I didn’t even really notice it.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.260
The only difference is that you have to plan
things ahead more

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.189
if you want to go out with your friends — you
plan in advance,

00:02:43.189 --> 00:02:46.400
or you say “I’ll meet up with them when
I meet up with them”.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.569
Things like that.

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:50.260
And many, many people have come and gone,

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:52.790
stayed for weeks or months at a time,

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:57.370
and really none of them have ever said
“man, I really miss my cell phone”.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:00.110
In the nearby town of Green Bank,
anything that transmits,

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:03.760
whether it’s baby monitors
or wireless doorbells, is banned.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.340
So is anything that might cause interference.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.840
Microwave ovens aren’t allowed.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:10.200
Power lines have to be buried
four feet underground.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:14.000
The observatory buildings near the telescope
are huge Faraday cages,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.640
keeping all the emissions inside.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:19.040
So this is the anechoic chamber
that I test in.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.760
I first test the device itself to see what
the emissions are,

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.029
then design a box for it,
put it back in the
chamber,

00:03:28.029 --> 00:03:29.760
and see if I did a good job or not.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:33.290
Things like cameras, for instance,

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:40.120
need to see high frequency
electromagnetic energy, namely light,

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:47.739
and yet we’re trying to keep them from emitting
lower frequency electromagnetic energy.

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:53.049
Usually we use a mesh embedded in glass
that’s very fine,

00:03:53.049 --> 00:03:57.549
and so it doesn’t distort the image too
much.

00:03:57.549 --> 00:03:59.239
Once you're within a mile of the telescope,

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:02.799
the restrictions are so severe that only
diesel cars are allowed.

00:04:02.799 --> 00:04:08.040
Regular gasoline cars: they cause too much
interference from the spark plugs inside.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.409
Folks who say they're hypersensitive to
electromagnetic fields move to this area:

00:04:12.409 --> 00:04:15.919
and even if pretty much all the evidence says
it's in their heads,

00:04:15.919 --> 00:04:18.090
they still feel better for being here.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:21.310
The scientists, however,
have more practical concerns.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:27.370
Mostly my work here deals with
radio-frequency interference on site,

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:31.410
and also doing routine runs to see
if there’s anything new out there.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:34.250
We have a monitoring station down on site,

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:35.819
and then we’ll jump in the truck.

00:04:35.819 --> 00:04:38.310
And we have direction finding equipment
in the truck.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:42.930
We just basically have to look at it with
the receiver and the spectrum analyser

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:46.480
and just drive around and watch
the signal peak or fall away.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:49.050
It is getting more difficult.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.199
Because there’s more and more
wireless stuff out there.

00:04:51.199 --> 00:04:53.860
The wireless genie is out of the bottle.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:55.900
And there’s so many things out there —

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:59.319
the Internet of Things is creeping into almost
every device.

00:04:59.319 --> 00:05:02.880
Refrigerators now have hotspots in them.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.490
When we go around and scan for wi-fi hotspots,

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:08.629
there’s always three or four printers out
there.

00:05:08.629 --> 00:05:13.580
The strangest one I’ve heard of
was an electric pad in a doghouse.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:15.169
There was some arcing inside the pad,

00:05:15.169 --> 00:05:17.889
and it was generating a lot of RFI, so…

00:05:17.889 --> 00:05:20.669
My predecessor’s the one that found that
one,

00:05:20.669 --> 00:05:26.349
and they fixed it by buying the guy a new
heater for the doghouse!

00:05:26.349 --> 00:05:28.600
Thank you to everyone at the
Green Bank Observatory

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.660
and all the staff at the radio telescope.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.409
I am kind of overwhelmed to be up here,

00:05:33.409 --> 00:05:35.360
I am so grateful to all of them,

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:36.430
please, pull down the description,

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:37.339
check out the links, and go see what they
do.

